From: MilkFlint <milkflint@localhost>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: flowtable: a software fast path for bridged
 connections

A connection a bridge forwards between two of its ports (a Wi-Fi client
and a wired one behind an access point) takes the bridge's whole receive
path for every frame, and with bridge conntrack, conntrack too. Add a flow
table type for the bridge family, nf_flow_table_bridge: nftables' "flow
add" in a bridge forward chain puts a connection into it, and its frames
then go from the port they arrive on straight to the port the bridge sent
the connection's frames to.

The fast path sits on the bridge's prerouting hook, right after the bridge
received a frame on a port and before conntrack, and sends the frame on as
it came: a bridge doesn't rewrite frames, so neither does the fast path
(no TTL or hop limit change, no new MAC header, no VLAN tags to pop or
push). It covers every bridge of the network namespace, so the flow table
needs no devices, and ports that join a bridge later (Wi-Fi interfaces,
which come and go) are covered without reloading the ruleset.

Only what it can do exactly as the bridge does is taken: untagged IPv4
and IPv6 TCP and UDP, without IPv4 options, fragments or IPv6 extension
headers, on a bridge without VLAN filtering, between two ports that
forward (STP), aren't locked (802.1X, MAB) and aren't isolated from each
other, to a unicast address the fdb has on the egress port (so never from
a flooded copy). Everything else goes the bridge's way.

A frame takes a flow only with the flow's ingress port and both of its MAC
addresses. One connection can cross a bridge more than once, as when a
router behind it routes between two subnets that both reach it through
the bridge; each crossing has frames of its own (other ports, other
addresses), and they go the bridge's way rather than taking another
crossing's flow. The ports and the bridge are checked on every frame: an
egress port that stops forwarding, leaves the bridge or is isolated ends
the flow, and an ingress port that doesn't forward, a bridge that starts
filtering VLANs or a dormant table sends the frame the bridge's way.

When the fdb moves an address to another port (a client roamed to another
access point) or drops it, the flows towards it are torn down at once:
the bridge tells switchdev of every fdb change, with or without hardware
under the ports, and the flow table listens. About once a second each
direction of a flow also sends a frame the bridge's own way. The bridge
keeps learning both addresses, so their fdb entries don't age out under a
busy flow and leave the bridge flooding, and the frame reaches the
forward chain, where nft_flow_offload hands it to the flow table's new
flow_check(): a flow whose frames the bridge now sends to another port is
torn down there too. The connection's next frames make a new one. A TCP
FIN or RST ends a flow as on the routed fast path.

nft_flow_offload takes the bridge family and, for a flow table type with
flow_alloc(), gets the flow from it, made from the packet in the forward
hook, instead of looking up a route; it refuses before allocating
anything what the fast path wouldn't take. There's no hardware offload: a bridge flow
table with "flags offload" is refused, as matching only the 5-tuple (as a
PPE does) can't tell one crossing from another. br_fdb_find_rcu() is
exported for the flow table's fdb check.

OpenWrt's pull request 24038 (a backport of Eric Woudstra's bridge
fastpath series for 6.18) does this through the forward path walk instead,
with hardware offload; this one is smaller, for 6.12, and differs where
that one was seen to go wrong: frames of another crossing take a flow
there (loops through a router behind the bridge), egress VLAN tags are
lost, TTLs are decremented, fdb entries age out under offloaded flows, and
Wi-Fi interfaces need a ruleset reload to be covered.

A KUnit suite (CONFIG_NF_FLOW_TABLE_BRIDGE_KUNIT_TEST) covers the packets
taken, the flows made and refused, what the fast path sends and how, and
when it leaves a frame to the bridge or ends a flow.

Signed-off-by: MilkFlint <milkflint@localhost>
---
--- a/include/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h
+++ b/include/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h
@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@
 struct nf_flowtable;
 struct nf_flow_rule;
 struct flow_offload;
+struct nft_pktinfo;
 enum flow_offload_tuple_dir;
 
 struct nf_flow_key {
@@ -64,6 +65,16 @@ struct nf_flowtable_type {
 	void				(*free)(struct nf_flowtable *ft);
 	void				(*get)(struct nf_flowtable *ft);
 	void				(*put)(struct nf_flowtable *ft);
+	/* A flowtable that doesn't route (the bridge's): nft_flow_offload
+	 * gets a new flow from flow_alloc() instead of a route (NULL: the
+	 * packet's connection can't have one), and shows flow_check() a
+	 * packet of a connection that already has a flow.
+	 */
+	struct flow_offload *		(*flow_alloc)(struct nf_conn *ct,
+						      const struct nft_pktinfo *pkt,
+						      enum ip_conntrack_dir dir);
+	void				(*flow_check)(struct nf_flowtable *ft,
+						      const struct nft_pktinfo *pkt);
 	nf_hookfn			*hook;
 	struct module			*owner;
 };
@@ -145,7 +156,14 @@ struct flow_offload_tuple {
 		};
 		struct {
 			u32		ifidx;
-			u32		hw_ifidx;
+			union {
+				u32	hw_ifidx;
+				/* a bridge flow (no hardware offload):
+				 * when a packet of this direction last
+				 * went through the bridge itself
+				 */
+				u32	br_checked;
+			};
 			u8		h_source[ETH_ALEN];
 			u8		h_dest[ETH_ALEN];
 		} out;
@@ -175,6 +193,7 @@ enum nf_flow_flags {
 enum flow_offload_type {
 	NF_FLOW_OFFLOAD_UNSPEC	= 0,
 	NF_FLOW_OFFLOAD_ROUTE,
+	NF_FLOW_OFFLOAD_BRIDGE,
 };
 
 struct flow_offload {
